[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what I changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The bug was a [1mbit-length vs byte-length mismatch[22m when calling [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m (and indirectly [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The BLAKE hash implementation uses an unusual convention: [38;5;10mblake512_update(state, data, datalen)[0m takes [38;5;10mdatalen[0m in [1mbits[22m, not bytes. The convenience function [38;5;10mblake512(out, in, inlen)[0m takes [38;5;10minlen[0m in [1mbytes[22m and internally multiplies by 8 before calling [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C code in two places calls [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m directly, passing [1mbyte counts[22m (not bit counts):[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. [1m[38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m[22m (KAT transcript functions) — e.g., [38;5;10mblakeX_update(ctx, p, n)[0m where [38;5;10mn = strlen(label)[0m[0m[0m
2. [1m[38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m[22m ([38;5;10mgen_message_random[0m, [38;5;10mhash_message[0m) — e.g., [38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N)[0m where [38;5;10mSPX_N = 32[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translation incorrectly "fixed" this by multiplying by 8 to convert to bits, e.g., [38;5;10mblake512_update(&mut s, data, (len as u64) * 8)[0m. But since the C reference code passes raw byte counts, the Rust code must do the same to produce identical output.[0m[0m
